// x-expr-walks.r2-06: a declaration inside a seq body is compiled as a
// value and refused with a message that contradicts the source.
//
// Machine::lower_block (graphix-types/src/expr/seq.rs:782) and
// Machine::lower_stmt (seq.rs:635) send every statement that is not a NoOp,
// a let or a connect through stmt_value, which binds it as a value
// (--expand shows `let seqv<id> = use str::len` under `let seqb<id>_0`).
// The compiler then refuses the `use` in value position
// (node/compiler.rs:390). The same happens to `type`, `trait`, `impl` and
// a static `mod`, at the seq level,
// in a `{ .. }` statement, in a let's or a connect's right-hand block, and
// in a try or with body. The book (core/seq.md, Statements) says that inside
// a block "you are back in ordinary Graphix" and that only `until` and `try`
// are refused there.
//
// command: timeout -s KILL 30 graphix --no-cache design/review-2026-10-05/repro/x-expr-walks.r2-06.gx
//          (--check is enough; the refusal is at compile time)
//
// expected: accepted, prints 3, exits (the same block outside the seq,
//   `let n = { use str::len; len("abc") }; n`, prints 3). If declarations
//   are not to be seq statements, a refusal that says so.
//
// observed:
//   0: at: line: 31, column: 25 .., in: use str::len
//   1: a use declaration is not an expression — it may only appear as a
//      statement in a block or module body, not where a value is expected
// with `type Q = i64; let z: Q = 3; z` as the block: "a type definition is
// not an expression — ..."; with `trait`: "a trait definition is not an
// expression — ..."; with `mod m;` at the seq level: "a module definition is
// not an expression — ...".
let r = seq { let n = { use str::len; len("abc") }; n };
sys::exit(sys::time::after_idle(duration:100.ms, 0));
r
